\description{
Opens the DSR Calculator, which calculates directly age-standardised rates
(DSRs) of emergency department presentations (ED) or admitted patient
separations (APC). It queries EpiServer for event counts by calendar year,
sex, 5-year age group and area of residence, matches them to the estimated
resident population of the same year, and standardises them to a chosen
standard population. Events can be limited to ICD-10 diagnosis codes and
rates to an age range. It shows counts, crude rates and DSRs, optionally
with age-specific rates, maps them by area, compares areas in an
interactive map and funnel plot, and copies R code that reproduces the
results. Its \emph{Help} tab sets out the data, formulas and sources.

Like \code{\link[=episerver_browse]{episerver_browse()}}, the calculator runs in a background R process by
default so that the console stays free, and it can be opened from the ACT
Epidemiology menu in the RStudio Addins menu. It opens in the system web
browser by default, so that it has the whole window: the settings are in a
sidebar on the left, in sections that open and close, and the results in
tabs on the top bar. The arrow at the top of the sidebar hides it.
}
\details{
\strong{Options}

The sidebar's sections hold the options: \emph{Events} (dataset, diagnosis
codes and search), \emph{Population} (population and geographic level), \emph{Years
and ages} (calendar years, pooling and ages) and \emph{Rates} (standard
population, rate per, suppression, male and female, and age-specific
rates).
\itemize{
\item \emph{Dataset}: ED presentations, counted by the calendar year of
\code{PresentationDateTime}, or APC separations, counted by the calendar year
of \code{SeparationDate}.
\item \emph{Population}: ACT residents, using the ACT population tables; ACT and
surrounds; or Australian residents, using the Australian tables. ACT and
surrounds covers the ACT and the four NSW SA3s that border it
(Queanbeyan, Snowy Mountains, Young - Yass and Tumut - Tumbarumba) with
their SA2s, and also uses the Australian tables. At state level it gives
two areas, the ACT and NSW (surrounds), each summed from its SA3s.
\item \emph{Geographic level}: state or territory (\code{geo_STATE}), SA3
(\code{geo_SA3_2021}) or SA2 (\code{geo_SA2_2021}) of residence, with rates for
each area. SA3s and SA2s are ASGS 2021 areas (see \strong{Data}). Populations
by 5-year age group are not available below SA2, so SA1 and mesh blocks
are not offered.
\item \emph{Standard population}: any standard with 5-year age groups in
\code{Analysis.dbo.StandardPops}. The default is the Australian 2001 standard
population.
\item \emph{Calendar years}: one row of results per year, or per period when the
years are pooled. The years offered are those covered by both the event
data and the population table, and a year only partly covered by the
event data is flagged. The slider starts on three years: the latest year
offered that the event data cover in full, and the two before it.
\item \emph{Pool years}: pools the years into periods of 2 to 5 years, or into one
period of all the selected years, for areas or conditions with few
events. The number of years selected must be a multiple of the period
length. Events and populations are summed over the years of a period, so
the rates are per person-year.
\item \emph{Ages}: limits the rates to a range of 5-year age groups, for example
under 65 (0-4 to 60-64), under 75 or 35 to 74. The default is all ages.
Events and populations outside the range are left out, and the DSR is
standardised within the range: each age group in it is weighted by its
share of the standard population of the range, so the weights sum to 1.
The crude rate is the events in the range divided by the population in
the range. Rates are per population of those ages, and can be compared
only with rates for the same ages and standard population.
\item \emph{Male and female}: adds male and female rates to the rates for persons.
\item \emph{Age-specific rates}: adds a table of rates by 5-year age group, for the
ages chosen.
\item \emph{Rate per}: 1,000, 10,000 or 100,000 population.
\item \emph{Suppress counts below}: rows with fewer events than this have their
events and rates withheld (see \code{\link[=dsr_calculate]{dsr_calculate()}}). The default is 5; 0
turns suppression off.
\item \emph{Diagnosis codes}: counts only events with a diagnosis matching any of
the ICD-10 codes or ranges entered, for example \code{J45}, \code{C13-C15.45} or
\verb{E18.3 to E18.78}. A partial code matches every code that starts with
it. A range runs from its first code to its last and includes every
code that starts with the last. Dots and case do not matter. Only
ICD-10 coded records are searched: ED records with an \code{ICD10AMEdition}
other than 99, and APC records with an \code{ICDVersion} of 10. With no codes
every event is counted.
\item \emph{Search}: for APC, the principal diagnosis (\code{Diagnosis1}) or any
diagnosis (\code{Diagnosis1} to \code{Diagnosis100}). ED always searches all four
of its diagnosis fields (\code{EDShortListCode} and \code{Diagnosis1} to
\code{Diagnosis3}), as \code{Diagnosis1} is recorded to 2020 and \code{EDShortListCode}
from 2013, so neither holds the principal diagnosis in every year.
}

\strong{Tables, maps and funnel plot}

The \emph{Tables} tab shows the rates and, when chosen, the age-specific
rates. \emph{Copy table} copies a table as tab-separated text that pastes into
Excel.

The \emph{Map} tab maps the age-standardised rate, crude rate, age-specific
rate or events of one year or period and sex by area. The age-specific
rate is for the age range chosen with \emph{Ages mapped}, within the ages of
the rates, for example 0 to 14 or 20 to 44: the events in the range
divided by the population in the range, suppressed as in the tables. It
is not age-standardised, so over a wide range it reflects differences in
age structure within the range; for a standardised rate, set \emph{Ages} and
map the age-standardised rate. Australia-wide SA3 and SA2 maps open on
the ACT and surrounding region; the view of Australia leaves out
Christmas, Cocos (Keeling) and Norfolk Islands. Boundaries are the ASGS
Edition 3 (2021) boundaries, downloaded from the ABS boundary service
(\code{geo.abs.gov.au}) the first time a level is mapped and kept in the folder
given by \code{tools::R_user_dir("actepir", "cache")}.
\emph{Copy map} copies the map as an image where the browser allows it; \emph{PNG}
and \emph{PDF} download it.

The \emph{Explore} tab shows an interactive map and a funnel plot side by
side, with the same settings as the \emph{Map} tab. Pointing at an area or a
point shows its figures. Clicking areas or points, or drawing round them
with the lasso in a plot's toolbar, selects them in both plots and lists
them below; \emph{Zoom to selected} zooms the map to them, and the magnifier in
the toolbar zooms and pans. The map can be coloured by value or by each
area's position in the funnel plot.

The funnel plot compares each area with the average of the areas plotted,
allowing for the size of its population, so that areas can be compared
without being misled by the chance variation of small numbers:
\itemize{
\item Crude and age-specific rates are plotted against the population.
\item DSRs are plotted against the population that would give the DSR as a
crude rate from the area's events (events / DSR), as in the funnel plots
of the Office for Health Improvement and Disparities (OHID). The average
is the events of all the areas over their derived populations. DSRs from
fewer than 10 events are not plotted.
\item Events are divided by the events expected from the age-specific rates of
all the areas plotted (indirect standardisation) and plotted against the
expected events, as counts alone cannot be compared between areas of
different sizes.
}

The curves are 95\% and 99.8\% control limits from exact Poisson tests of
each area's events against the events expected at the average: an area
outside them differs from the average by more than chance. When areas
vary more than chance alone would explain (overdispersion), as large
counts such as all ED presentations often do, most areas fall outside
these limits. \emph{Allow for overdispersion}, ticked by default, then widens
the limits by Spiegelhalter's additive random effects method, estimated
from z-scores on the square root scale winsorised at the 10th and 90th
percentiles, so that areas outside them stand out from the usual variation
between areas. The tab reports the overdispersion ratio and its
chi-squared test; with fewer than 10 areas the estimate is unreliable.
Suppressed areas are neither plotted nor included in the average. Areas
above the limits are coral (99.8\%) and yellow (95\%), and areas below them
teal (99.8\%) and blue (95\%), from the ACT Government's regional colours.

The \emph{Help} tab gives the formulas for each rate, confidence interval and
funnel plot limit, the tables and fields used, the boundary services and
references.

\strong{Data}

Age groups (0-4, 5-9, ..., 80-84, 85+) are formed from age in years
(\code{AgeYrs} in ED, \code{AgeYears} in APC) and match \code{AgeGroup05Code} in the
population tables. Populations come from the 5-year estimated resident
population tables (\code{ERP5_STE}, \code{ERP5_SA3} and \code{ERP5_SA2}, ACT or
Australian) for the same year as the events. Area of residence comes from
the geocoded \code{geo_} columns; for ACT and surrounds, the state comes from
the SA3 (\code{geo_SA3_2021}).

SA3s and SA2s are areas of the Australian Statistical Geography Standard
(ASGS) Edition 3 (2021). The population tables hold areas of this
edition, as do the \code{geo_SA3_2021} and \code{geo_SA2_2021} columns and the map
boundaries. The tables do not record their edition, so the calculator
assumes ASGS 2021 and labels SA3 and SA2 results and maps with it. Area
codes and boundaries differ between editions, so populations on ASGS 2016
or ASGS 2026 areas would not match these.

Events with no geocoded residence, events in an area missing from the
population table and events with no recorded age are left out of the
rates, and the calculator reports how many there are. See
\code{\link[=dsr_calculate]{dsr_calculate()}} for how the rates and their 95\% confidence intervals are
calculated.

\strong{Code}

The code button (\emph{Copy Code} in background mode, \emph{Insert Code} in
foreground mode) gives R code containing the three SQL queries the
calculator runs, including any diagnosis condition, the lines that keep
the age groups in range when the ages are limited and that assign each
year to its period when the years are pooled, and the
\code{\link[=dsr_calculate]{dsr_calculate()}} call, with the suppression threshold, that turns their
results into the table shown. The code reproduces the results outside the
calculator and can be adapted, for example by adding conditions to the
events query.
}
